From: OpenSec <contactus@opensec.in>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 05:34:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AOT: endsWith() of a string of 65,535 characters or more in
 compact code

In compact code, s.endsWith(literal) takes the characters and length of the
string from Stub::NarrowCharacters, which saturates the length at 0xffff. The
literal was then compared with the characters before that position instead
of with the end of the string, so the answer for a longer string was wrong.
Such a string now goes to operationStringEndsWith. (startsWith() needs no
more than the length it was given; the other users of the stub compare the
length with literals shorter than 0xffff.)

Found by comparing Pi's tool results with a stock Bun's: the tail of a long
command output kept an empty last line, since `text.endsWith("\n")` said no.
---
 Source/JavaScriptCore/aot/AOTLowerBuiltins.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/JavaScriptCore/aot/AOTLowerBuiltins.cpp b/Source/JavaScriptCore/aot/AOTLowerBuiltins.cpp
index 8a4d3a1c0d..ed0c23c528 100644
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/aot/AOTLowerBuiltins.cpp
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/aot/AOTLowerBuiltins.cpp
@@ -574,6 +574,13 @@ bool Lowering::lowerCallOfBuiltin(Node* node, Node* calleeNode, unsigned argc, u
             Vector<ValueFromBlock, 2> lengthsOtherwise;
             Vector<ValueFromBlock, 3> answers;
             auto [characters, length] = narrowCharactersOf(thisValue, notForTheLooking, lengthsOtherwise);
+            if (!atStart && isCompact()) {
+                // In compact code the length is saturated at 0xffff (Stub::NarrowCharacters): where a longer string ends is not known.
+                LBasicBlock lengthIsKnown = m_out.newBlock();
+                lengthsOtherwise.append(m_out.anchor(length));
+                m_out.branch(m_out.aboveOrEqual(length, m_out.constInt32(0xffff)), rarely(notForTheLooking), usually(lengthIsKnown));
+                m_out.appendTo(lengthIsKnown);
+            }
             LValue needed = m_out.constInt32(written->length());
             answers.append(m_out.anchor(m_out.booleanFalse));
             m_out.branch(m_out.aboveOrEqual(length, needed), unsure(isLongEnough), unsure(settled));
